WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.625
SCOPE, from 2024

2
00:00:01.703 --> 00:00:04.352
counts small patterns around every

3
00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:06.300
vertex of a large graph.

4
00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:08.698
Take our graph of forty vertices

5
00:00:08.762 --> 00:00:10.490
and a hundred twenty edges.

6
00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:11.806
At vertex one

7
00:00:11.875 --> 00:00:14.292
there are seventy-eight wedges with

8
00:00:14.361 --> 00:00:16.086
the vertex at the center

9
00:00:16.086 --> 00:00:17.467
fourteen triangles

10
00:00:17.467 --> 00:00:19.400
and fifty-five four-cycles.

11
00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.780
Counting copies one by one is slow.

12
00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:23.612
Instead

13
00:00:23.612 --> 00:00:26.081
two shared tables serve the whole batch

14
00:00:26.081 --> 00:00:28.730
the degrees and the common-neighbor counts.

15
00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:31.199
Wedges centered at a vertex are

16
00:00:31.268 --> 00:00:32.840
pairs of its neighbors.

17
00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:35.782
Triangles at a vertex come from the neighbors

18
00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:37.510
it shares with each neighbor.

19
00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.365
And a wedge end is a walk of two steps

20
00:00:40.365 --> 00:00:42.420
minus the walks that come back.

21
00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:45.564
On our graph, each formula matches brute

22
00:00:45.633 --> 00:00:47.170
force at every vertex.

23
00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:50.310
The task removes SCOPE's batch mode.

24
00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.722
Each submission counts twenty-four five-vertex

25
00:00:53.789 --> 00:00:55.852
patterns and four smaller ones

26
00:00:55.852 --> 00:00:57.317
on three real graphs

27
00:00:57.317 --> 00:00:59.380
and every count must be exact.

28
00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:02.388
Claude Opus 5.5 wrote each count as a

29
00:01:02.459 --> 00:01:04.901
signed sum of homomorphism counts

30
00:01:04.901 --> 00:01:07.343
maps that need not be one-to-one

31
00:01:07.343 --> 00:01:09.570
which are far easier to count.

32
00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:12.240
Five of sixteen models passed.

33
00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:14.629
The reference takes three point

34
00:01:14.695 --> 00:01:16.537
five nine seconds per batch

35
00:01:16.537 --> 00:01:18.839
and Gemini 4 Argon, running in Pi

36
00:01:18.839 --> 00:01:21.470
takes zero point one two three seconds.

37
00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:23.878
ScaleGPM, also from 2024

38
00:01:23.951 --> 00:01:26.072
estimates pattern counts with

39
00:01:26.145 --> 00:01:27.900
two approximate engines

40
00:01:27.973 --> 00:01:29.801
neighborhood sampling and

41
00:01:29.874 --> 00:01:31.410
graph sparsification.

42
00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:33.627
Which is faster depends on the

43
00:01:33.689 --> 00:01:35.120
graph and the settings.

44
00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:37.223
The agent writes the function

45
00:01:37.283 --> 00:01:38.190
that picks one.

46
00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:41.446
The score weighs each pick by its regret

47
00:01:41.446 --> 00:01:44.140
its runtime over the faster engine's.

48
00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:46.257
On twelve made-up settings

49
00:01:46.323 --> 00:01:48.155
two selectors are each right

50
00:01:48.221 --> 00:01:49.530
ten times in twelve.

51
00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:51.975
One is wrong on two close calls

52
00:01:51.975 --> 00:01:54.200
and scores ninety-one point five.

53
00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:56.871
The other is wrong on two wide gaps

54
00:01:56.871 --> 00:01:58.870
and scores eighteen point two.

55
00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:01.966
Claude Opus 5.5 predicted each engine's

56
00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:03.707
time with its own formula

57
00:02:03.772 --> 00:02:06.223
fitted to its own timings on generated

58
00:02:06.287 --> 00:02:07.513
small-world graphs

59
00:02:07.513 --> 00:02:09.770
and picked the smaller prediction.

60
00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:11.022
Its own idea

61
00:02:11.022 --> 00:02:13.936
it read locality from the triangle count

62
00:02:13.936 --> 00:02:16.920
because local neighbor lists merge faster.

63
00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:19.222
Seven of sixteen models passed

64
00:02:19.222 --> 00:02:20.985
and Opus scored ninety-two

65
00:02:21.048 --> 00:02:22.559
against eighty-six point

66
00:02:22.622 --> 00:02:24.070
nine for the reference.

67
00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:24.846
So

68
00:02:24.846 --> 00:02:27.591
count from tables the whole batch shares

69
00:02:27.591 --> 00:02:30.010
and turn hard counts into easy ones.

70
00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:32.783
And when a pick is scored by regret

71
00:02:32.783 --> 00:02:34.600
the wide gaps matter most.

72
00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:37.921
As an exercise, derive the wedge-end formula

73
00:02:37.989 --> 00:02:39.271
from homomorphisms

74
00:02:39.271 --> 00:02:42.310
and say which maps the minus degree removes.

75
00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:46.100
Next, checkpoints, branches and versions.
